            'title' => 'What the big nephrology congress of 2026 brought: five questions we still cannot answer',
            'image_alt' => 'Deep purple background with a translucent teal kidney in the centre and five glowing dots around it, clean illustration without text.',
            'excerpt' => 'In early November 2026, a major international nephrology congress delivered five stories that caught the attention of clinical practice: from SGLT2 inhibitors in octogenarians through pregnancy after kidney donation to anaemia on dialysis, hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis. None of them has a simple answer. What unites them is one thing: the individual patient decides, not age or diagnosis.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>In early November 2026, a major international nephrology congress, together with the first professional summaries, brought five stories that caught the attention of clinical practice. They concern SGLT2 inhibitors in people over 80, pregnancy after kidney donation, a new drug for anaemia on dialysis, a potassium binder in hyperkalaemia, and fruit and vegetables in metabolic acidosis. <a href="https://nefro.polascin.net/article.php?slug=kidney-week-2026-sglt2-darcovstvo-anemia-hyperkaliemia">A detailed professional analysis with all the cited studies</a> can be found on the Nefro-projekt.</p>
<p>The first story is about SGLT2 inhibitors in people over 80 with chronic kidney disease. A retrospective analysis of a large international database of health records brought favourable associations with mortality and with composite cardiac and kidney outcomes, but its two public versions disagree: one reports 23,765 patients in each group after matching, the other, in the organisers' press release, 10,076 people and different estimates, including a higher incidence of some adverse events. Until this discrepancy is explained, the data cannot be pooled, nor can a new safety profile of the drugs be derived from them. Moreover, an association with lower mortality does not by itself prove a causal effect.</p>
<p>Randomised data for older people do exist, however. A large international trial of dapagliflozin enrolled 4,304 patients, including octogenarians, and found no convincing evidence that the effect of the drug differed by age. Age alone should therefore not decide against treatment. The choice should respect the indication, kidney function, albuminuria, heart failure and the patient's overall condition. Professional recommendations also distinguish the expected temporary drop in estimated filtration after starting treatment from a reason to stop it, and advise considering a temporary pause during prolonged fasting, surgery or critical illness.</p>
<p>The second story concerns pregnancy after kidney donation. A Canadian cohort study published in late October 2026 in a major medical journal compared 385 pregnancies in 252 donors with 1,540 matched pregnancies in 1,535 women who had not donated a kidney. Hospitalisation for gestational hypertension, pre-eclampsia or eclampsia was recorded in 11.9% of donors versus 8.3% in the control group, with a risk ratio of 1.51 and a 95% confidence interval from 1.10 to 2.08. The difference in observed proportions is 3.6 percentage points: a risk ratio of 1.51 does not mean 51 percentage points higher absolute risk. Most pregnancies after kidney donation proceeded without complications. The result supports thorough counselling before donation and taking donation history into account in prenatal care; it does not justify blanket refusal for donors planning pregnancy.</p>
<p>The third story comes from a study of a drug for anaemia in dialysis. A pragmatic randomised trial across 161 dialysis units compared vadadustat given three times a week with epoetin alfa; 2,116 patients were randomised. The composite outcome of death and hospitalisation was favourable, mainly thanks to fewer hospitalisations, but mortality itself was not shown to be lower: its ratio was 1.00 with a 95% confidence interval from 0.72 to 1.39. These are preliminary congress results. Older published trials showed at most comparable cardiovascular safety versus darbepoetin alfa, not superiority. According to the European regulatory summary, the approved dosing of vadadustat is once daily, so a regimen of three times a week from the trial is not automatically a new approved dosing. Current recommendations for anaemia in chronic kidney disease in any case prefer erythropoiesis-stimulating agents over HIF-PH inhibitors; the new congress data are a reason for further evaluation, not for an immediate change of practice.</p>
<p>The fourth story concerns potassium and the dietary burden. An open-label randomised trial in 196 haemodialysis patients with hyperkalaemia examined what a potassium binder adds to standardised nutritional counselling. After eight weeks, the quality-of-life score in the binder group was 3.8 points better, with a 95% confidence interval from 2.7 to 4.9 points, and the pre-dialysis potassium level was lower. For the patient this means a less restrictive diet. Eight weeks of follow-up, however, does not prove a long-term effect on heart rhythm disorders or mortality, and it does not authorise unrestricted potassium intake.</p>
<p>The fifth story examined fruit and vegetables in metabolic acidosis. A ten-year analysis of 108 patients with stage G3 chronic kidney disease without diabetes compared fruit and vegetables for reducing acid load with sodium bicarbonate and with usual care. Kidney replacement therapy was started in four patients in the fruit-and-vegetables group versus sixteen in usual care, and fewer were hospitalised, but total ten-year costs did not differ between the groups. A slower cost growth in a model is therefore not a proven saving over the whole period. Moreover, a total plasma CO<sub>2</sub> of 22 to 24 mmol/l is not automatically severe metabolic acidosis; the recommendations cite a value below 18 mmol/l as the example of severe acidosis.</p>
<p>What do these stories mean for everyday practice? They refine decision-making; they do not replace it with a single uniform protocol. In an older patient, the choice of inhibitor should follow the verified indication and overall condition, not age itself. In a potential donor, later pregnancy should also be discussed. In anaemia, the approved regimen must be distinguished from the one studied. In hyperkalaemia, potassium level, nutritional quality of the diet and the daily treatment burden must be weighed together. A less restrictive diet has clinical value only when safety and adequate nutrition are maintained.</p>
<p>The big congress thus brought five good questions, not five ready answers, and that is precisely its message: treat the person, not the number in the statistics.</p>
<p><em>This article is for information only and does not replace individual medical advice. Your treating physician decides on specific treatment based on your test results and the medicines you take. Names of persons, company names and other identifying details of third parties are deliberately anonymised in this text; the original citations can be found in the linked professional article.</em></p>

